{{Short description|none}}
Sports in [[Okaiken]] are a defining element of Sanese culture, characterized by a profound reverence for physical combat, individual fortitude, and a nationwide dedication to physical fitness. Unlike many neighboring nations, the sporting landscape in Okaiken is dominated by heavy-contact team sports, martial arts, and bodybuilding. The national hierarchy of popular sports is led by [[w:Rugby union|Rugby]] and [[Lurandian Football]], followed by [[w:Association football|Association football]], martial arts, [[w:Boxing|boxing]], [[w:Tennis|tennis]], and lastly, [[w:Baseball|baseball]].

Going to the gym, fitness training, and bodybuilding are officially the number one hobbies in Okaiken. This cultural phenomenon, often referred to as the {{nihongo|Cult of Iron|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}}, permeates all levels of society and is heavily supported by state initiatives.

==History==
===Early history and Republic Era===
[[File:Motonobu_Statue_Mikata.png|thumb|right|250px|A statue of [[Anjō Motonobu]] in [[Mikata, Miyagi|Mikata]]. His legendary physical strength and battlefield endurance serve as the historical foundation for Okaiken's modern Cult of Iron.]]
Historical sports in Okaiken were deeply tied to martial applications and religious ceremonies. The origins of the nation's reverence for physical strength can be traced back to the Sengoku period, epitomized by legendary figures such as [[Anjō Motonobu]]. Motonobu, a daimyō and key vassal to [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]], was renowned for his massive physical stature and immense raw power, famously fighting without shoulder armor to maximize his mobility and strength in naval and close-quarters combat. His physical feats, such as lifting entire logs unassisted during the [[Battle of Morihama]] and surviving the freezing currents of the [[Sai River]] despite mortal wounds, established a historical precedent for the "Iron Body" ideal long before it became state policy.

Traditional martial arts such as {{nihongo|[[w:Judo|Judo]]|柔道|Jūdō}}, {{nihongo|Takeuchi-ryu|竹内流|Takeuchi-ryū}}, and {{nihongo|[[w:Karate|Karate]]|空手|Karate}} were developed alongside these martial feats as both combat disciplines and spiritual practices. {{nihongo|[[w:Sumo|Sumo]]|相撲|Sumō}} retains a ceremonial role, particularly tied to the [[Imperial House of Okaiken]], but modern public interest has shifted heavily toward full-contact sports.

During the Republic Era, western sports were introduced, but the rugged individualism cultivated during this time led the populace to favor sports that emphasized physical resilience. {{nihongo|[[w:Baseball|Baseball]]|野球|Yakyū}} was introduced during the Saito Restoration but was largely rejected by the post-war generation as too slow and non-confrontational.

===Post-Jutsu Restoration (2011-present)===
Following the Jutsu Restoration of 2011, the {{nihongo|Forge State|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} ideology heavily influenced Sanese sporting culture. Physical culture became intertwined with national identity. The government has actively promoted fitness as a matter of national security and public health.

==Physical Culture and Bodybuilding==
[[File:Kazuki_Hino_Mr_Adonia_2007.png|thumb|left|250px|[[Kazuki Hino]], an 8-time Mr. Adonia Champion.]]
Fitness training and [[w:Bodybuilding|bodybuilding]] constitute the most popular hobby and recreational activity in Okaiken. Gymnasiums are ubiquitous across major cities, and the cultural ideal emphasizes a muscular and imposing physique. Major domestic events like the Okaiken National Grand Prix and the Iron Sakura Classic draw massive crowds and extensive media coverage.

This national obsession is supported by the highest levels of government. [[Crown Prince Tomohito]] launched the National Iron Body Initiative ({{nihongo|Kokka Tesshin Keikaku|国家鉄身計画}}), a state-sponsored framework encouraging youth to engage in [[w:Weightlifting|weightlifting]], [[w:Rugby union|rugby]], and martial arts to build a resilient citizenry. Similarly, [[Rento Nakayama]], the Minister of Health and Welfare and a former professional men's physique competitor, has championed the democratization of wellness. His platform links physical fitness to preventative healthcare and national welfare, making fitness a core pillar of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]]'s social agenda.

Okaiken has produced world-renowned bodybuilders, most notably [[Kazuki Hino]], known as The Iron Monk. Hino won the prestigious [[Mr. Adonia]] title eight consecutive times from 2007 to 2015, popularizing the Mass Monster era in Okaiken and inspiring a generation of Sanese citizens to pursue extreme physical development through his high-intensity Total Annihilation training philosophy. During his reign, his most bitter rivalry was with [[Satoshi Endo|Satoshi "The Granite" Endo]], a competitor known for his unparalleled conditioning and striated glutes. Their fierce posedowns at the 2011 and 2012 Mr. Adonia competitions are legendary, sparking nationwide debates over the ideal of mass versus conditioning. The women's physique division is currently dominated by [[Yoko Shirai]], a former gymnast whose transition into mass-building has made her a national icon for female empowerment within the Forge State. She frequently battles her long-time rival, [[Maki Sonoda|Maki "The Valkyrie" Sonoda]], in the Iron Sakura Classic.

===Goriki===
{{further|Goriki}}
[[File:Goriki_Kure_Classic.png|thumb|right|250px|A [[Goriki]] competitor performing the Motonobu Log Lift at the Kure Heavy Industries Strongman Classic.]]
Tying directly into the Forge State ideology and the national obsession with bodybuilding, {{nihongo|'''[[Goriki]]'''|強力|Gōriki}} is Okaiken's localized version of [[w:Strongman (strength athlete)|Strongman]] competitions. The sport traces its spiritual lineage directly to Sengoku-era warriors like [[Anjō Motonobu]], who were celebrated for their functional, battlefield strength. Rather than standard weights, competitors lift and move objects tied to the nation's heavy industry and maritime history. 

Events at the premier Kure Heavy Industries Strongman Classic include dragging ship anchors, loading heavy industrial steel cogs, the traditional lifting of massive shrine stones, and the Motonobu Log Lift, a grueling overhead press event that serves as a direct homage to Motonobu's legendary feat of strength at the [[Battle of Morihama]]. Goriki competitors are national icons of raw power, often sharing the spotlight with professional bodybuilders. The current undisputed champion is [[Goro Takada]], affectionately known as The Anvil, who recently set a national record by pulling a decommissioned coastal patrol boat for fifty meters. Takada's absolute dominance is frequently challenged by [[Daiki Hoshino]], a former dockworker whose explosive lifting speed creates a thrilling contrast to Takada's grinding, methodical power.

==Professional organizations and popularity==
Due to the pervasive nature of the Cult of Iron and the high social value placed on physical endurance, the Sanese public is exceptionally sports-savvy. The demographic of citizens who do not follow or care about sports is remarkably low compared to global averages, steadily declining since the Jutsu Restoration. Professional sports leagues reflecting high-contact, physical combat, and strength dominate television ratings and live attendance.

